window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][10] = "<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> India's Constitution lists 22 scheduled languages, and participants may be far more at ease in one of them than in English. Run interviews, screeners and surveys in the participant's own language, with a moderator or interpreter who speaks it, and test a translated script aloud, because a translated question can change its meaning <code>Inferred</code>. Consent is a language question too: India's data protection law requires that a notice, and a request for consent, can be accessed in English or any of those 22 languages (sections 5(3) and 6(3) of the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023) <code>[V: the Act's text as reproduced by dpdpa.com and law-firm summaries]</code>, with its main obligations due from 13 May 2027 (see the Dates box in C01), so offer a research consent form the same way. Read the current rules yourself at the time you read this book <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-6-quantitative-research-surveys-analytics-sampling-and-b\"></span>\n<h4>C06.6 Quantitative research: surveys, analytics, sampling and bias</h4>\n<p>Quantitative research answers &quot;how many, how much, how often?&quot; It measures prevalence; it does not explain why.</p>\n<p><strong>Analytics</strong> show what people did, at scale: where they stop in a funnel, how often they return, which features they use (<a href=\"#gl-funnel\">funnel</a>, <a href=\"#gl-retention\">retention</a>, C05). They are strong on behavior and silent on reasons. Pair them with interviews that explain the numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Surveys</strong> ask many people the same questions. FW-C06-05 is a checklist. Principles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start from the decision: what will you do differently depending on the answers?</li>\n<li>One idea per question; neutral wording; balanced answer options (avoid &quot;How great was...&quot;); include &quot;don't know.&quot;</li>\n<li>Ask about behavior (&quot;how many times did you order last month?&quot;) more than intention (&quot;will you order more?&quot;).</li>\n<li>Put sensitive and demographic questions last. Keep it short; length lowers completion.</li>\n<li>Pilot it with five people before sending.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sampling and non-response.</strong> Who answers is not who exists. A survey sent to current users misses people who left (survivorship, <a href=\"#gl-base-rate\">base rate</a> and related ideas in C12). People who respond are often those with strong opinions. State who is missing and which way it biases the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Margin of error.</strong> For a proportion from a random sample, the 95% margin of error in the worst case is about 1 divided by the square root of the sample size. With 400 responses it is about ±5 percentage points, with 1,000 about ±3, and with 100 about ±10. A 52% versus 48% split on 400 people is not a difference you can act on. The reasoning is developed in C12 (<a href=\"#gl-confidence-interval\">confidence interval</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Combining methods.</strong> Use qualitative work to find what to ask, quantitative work to size it, and behavioral data to check what people said. When they disagree, the behavior usually wins (C06.7).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use surveys.</strong> When you do not yet know the right questions (use interviews), when the audience is tiny, or when behavior is observable (look at the data instead).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team sends a 30-question survey to 200 customers to &quot;see what they want.&quot; The response rate is 12%, the answers are a mix of wishes and complaints, and nobody can say what decision they inform. A survey without a decision and a question set grounded in prior interviews produces numbers and no direction.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-7-the-say-do-gap-and-signal-versus-noise-in-feedback\"></span>\n<h4>C06.7 The say-do gap, and signal versus noise in feedback</h4>\n<p>The <strong>say-do gap</strong> is the difference between what people say they do or want and what they actually do. <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a> is the reason intentions are poor evidence. People say they would pay, exercise, read the terms, and use the new feature, and then do not.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes.</strong> People want to be agreeable; they cannot predict their own future behavior; they remember badly; they do not know what drives their choices; and hypothetical costs feel lighter than real ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Narrowing the gap.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prefer <strong>behavior to statements</strong>: what they did, not what they plan to do (revealed preference).</li>\n<li>Ask about <strong>specific past events</strong>, not general habits or future intentions.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>commitment tests</strong>: something that costs the user a little (an email address, a deposit, a pre-order, ten minutes) is a stronger signal than a stated intention.</li>\n<li><strong>Triangulate:</strong> compare interviews, a survey and the data. Where they disagree, ask why.</li>\n<li><strong>Discount stated intentions</strong> with an explicit factor when you must use them, and test the factor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The say-do gap is large for future behavior and hypotheticals and small for simple facts (device, role, team size) <code>Inferred</code>. Do not discard everything people say; discount the right things.</p>\n<p><strong>Signal versus noise in feedback.</strong> Not all feedback is equally informative. Each source has a bias.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What it tells you</th>\n<th>Bias</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Support tickets</td>\n<td>Problems that blocked someone</td>\n<td>Misses silent frustration; over-represents bugs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App-store or public reviews</td>\n<td>Strong reactions</td>\n<td>Extremes; small share of users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sales and lost-deal notes</td>\n<td>Objections in buying</td>\n<td>Second-hand; rationalized reasons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social media and forums</td>\n<td>Vocal communities</td>\n<td>Not representative; loud is not many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power-user requests</td>\n<td>Needs of the heaviest users</td>\n<td>Not typical of new or casual users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interviews</td>\n<td>Depth and reasons</td>\n<td>Small samples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior data</td>\n<td>What happened at scale</td>\n<td>Does not explain why</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Weighting.</strong> Weigh feedback by how many people in the target segment, how severe, and how consistent across sources (FW-C06-06). One loud customer is a data point; the same issue in tickets, interviews and behavior is a pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>The test.</strong> Ask: &quot;What would make me change my mind?&quot; If nothing would, you are collecting evidence for a conclusion you already hold.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team discounts all survey responses because of the say-do gap and ignores a clear complaint pattern in support tickets. The complaint is about something that already happened to users, so it is behavior, not intention. The gap applies to predictions about the future; reports of past problems are good evidence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-8-writing-a-problem-statement-a-team-can-act-on\"></span>\n<h4>C06.8 Writing a problem statement a team can act on</h4>\n<p>A <strong>problem statement</strong> is a short description of who has what problem in what context and why it matters, written without a solution. <a href=\"#gl-problem-statement\">problem statement</a> is the written output of discovery. It aligns a team on what to solve before it argues about how.</p>\n<p>The template from C02 (FW-C02-01) has five rows: who, what happens today, why it matters, evidence, and what is not the problem. A statement built from it reads: &quot;New users who sign up from a mobile ad abandon setup at the permissions screen because they cannot see why the app needs access, so only 41% reach a first lesson.&quot; (All figures in this chapter's examples are invented.)</p>\n<p><strong>Tests for a good statement.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>It names a specific segment and situation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>It describes behavior or an observable outcome,</strong> not a feeling alone.</li>\n<li><strong>It states the impact,</strong> ideally as a <a href=\"#gl-metric\">metric</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>It cites the evidence and how strong it is.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>It contains no solution words.</strong> If it uses &quot;needs a wizard&quot; or &quot;should have a button,&quot; it is a solution in disguise.</li>\n<li><strong>It is solvable at the team's level:</strong> not so broad (&quot;improve onboarding&quot;) that nothing follows, and not so narrow that it is one option.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Weak</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n<th>Strong</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Users need a better dashboard&quot;</td>\n<td>A solution; no user or evidence</td>\n<td>&quot;Account managers preparing a monthly review spend about two hours copying figures into slides, and 6 of 8 interviewed do it by hand&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Onboarding is confusing&quot;</td>\n<td>A judgment; no segment</td>\n<td>&quot;First-time admins at companies under 50 people stop at the integration step; 38% finish setup within a week, against 70% for larger accounts&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;We should make it social&quot;</td>\n<td>A solution</td>\n<td>&quot;Learners who study alone lapse after three weeks; those who study with a friend are more likely to continue (a correlation to test)&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>When not to use it.</strong> For a cheap, reversible, obvious change; the statement would be longer than the fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> A statement can be polished and wrong. Write the evidence line honestly, including &quot;low confidence: two interviews.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-9-the-cheapest-test-that-could-falsify-the-riskiest-assu\"></span>\n<h4>C06.9 The cheapest test that could falsify the riskiest assumption</h4>\n<p>Every idea rests on assumptions. A <strong>hypothesis</strong> from C03 states one in testable form. The discipline is to find the assumption that is both important and uncertain, and to test it as cheaply as possible, in a way that could prove you wrong. FW-C06-07 is the routine. (The wider practice of assumption mapping and the minimum viable product is in C15.)</p>\n<p><strong>Four kinds of assumption</strong> (a widely used grouping in design and product practice): desirability (do people want this?), usability (can they use it?), feasibility (can we build it?) and viability (does it work for the business?). Many failed products had a desirability assumption that was never tested <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cheap tests, from least to most costly.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>What it measures</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conversation</td>\n<td>Whether the problem is real and how people cope today</td>\n<td>Eight interviews about the last time they did the task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fake door or smoke test</td>\n<td>Whether people take a step toward it</td>\n<td>A button for the feature that leads to a &quot;coming soon&quot; page with a sign-up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Landing page</td>\n<td>Interest from a defined audience</td>\n<td>A page describing the offer and counting sign-ups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concierge test</td>\n<td>Whether the outcome is valued, delivered by hand</td>\n<td>Coordinating ten group orders manually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prototype test</td>\n<td>Whether people can use the design</td>\n<td>Five people complete tasks on a clickable prototype</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-order or deposit</td>\n<td>Willingness to pay</td>\n<td>A deposit or a refundable pre-order</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rules.</strong> Choose the assumption whose failure would sink the idea. Write the success criterion before the test (&quot;at least 6 of 10 complete a group order with three or more people&quot;), because criteria set afterwards bend toward the result. Decide what you will do in each outcome. Prefer tests that measure behavior over opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to test.</strong> When building is cheaper than testing and the change is reversible (<a href=\"#gl-reversible-decision\">reversible decision</a>, C02), or when a decision does not depend on the result.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team runs a three-week fake-door test for a button that takes half a day to build and can be removed with a flag. The test cost more than the experiment would have. Reserve tests for assumptions that are expensive to be wrong about.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c06-c06-10-working-with-researchers\"></span>\n<h4>C06.10 Working with researchers</h4>\n<p>User researchers are specialists in studying people. Treat them as partners in framing the question, not as a service that produces slides after you have decided.</p>\n<p><strong>What researchers contribute.</strong> Framing the question; choosing the method; recruiting the right participants; moderating without leading; analyzing; and telling you what the evidence cannot support.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of research they run:</strong> generative (what problems exist), evaluative (does this design work), survey-based (how common), and behavioral analysis. Experiments (C14) are different, and are usually run with data science.</p>\n<p><strong>Where PMs go wrong.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asking for research to confirm a decision already made.</li>\n<li>Joining after the questions are fixed.</li>\n<li>Generalizing from five interviews.</li>\n<li>Treating a quotation as a finding.</li>\n<li>Hoarding the results instead of sharing the decision they changed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A good partnership.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Involve the researcher before the question is set. Write the research questions together, and the decision each informs.</li>\n<li>Attend sessions. Watching a person struggle is worth more than reading a summary of it.</li>\n<li>Agree up front what would change the plan.</li>\n<li>Share what you decided and why, so the researcher sees the effect.</li>\n<li>Respect the limits they name.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Where a company has no researcher, run the same discipline yourself: write the decision and the questions first, use neutral prompts, and record sessions.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The problem-definition template in C02 (FW-C02-01) and the hypothesis card from C03 (FW-C03-04) are reused rather than repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-01 · The three-layer ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a request, complaint or idea stated as a feature or solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the problem (need, situation, workaround), the solution class, and the feature, with the problem stated separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can reach the person or evidence about their situation; the request has a need behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> understanding versus time; stepping up can feel like deflection.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> inventing a deeper problem; reframing to your preferred solution; not confirming the reframing with the requester.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;add bedtime mode&quot; in a children's video app steps up to &quot;parents struggle to end screen time without a fight,&quot; with several solution classes (Worked example 1).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) write the request verbatim; (2) ask what they are trying to do; (3) ask what happens and what they do today; (4) write the problem in one sentence with no solution words; (5) list two or three solution classes; (6) confirm the problem with the requester.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-02 · The segment selection scorecard · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> two to four candidate segments and what you know about each.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a scored comparison on size, pain intensity, reach and fit, a choice, and what is given up.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you have rough evidence on each criterion; scores can be rough (low, medium, high).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a quick scorecard is coarse; weighting hides tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> choosing the largest segment by default; scoring to justify a favorite; ignoring reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for a language-learning app, three segments (students, professionals, retirees) are scored and the app picks professionals preparing for a move abroad (Worked example 2 notes the method; IQ-C06-02 works the full example).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Pain intensity</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Fit and right to win</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>State the deciding criterion: &quot;I choose B because its pain is most intense and we can reach it directly, and I give up A's larger size.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-03 · The job statement and job map · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> jobs to be done as developed by Clayton Christensen and colleagues, and Anthony Ulwick's outcome-driven innovation <code>[V: Christensen and colleagues (HBR 2016); Ulwick's outcome-driven innovation]</code>; the &quot;When... I want... so I can...&quot; statement format is common in practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> interviews or observations of people doing a task.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a job statement (situation, motivation, outcome), the functional, emotional and social dimensions, the steps of the job, the current alternatives, and the struggles.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can observe the situation and the alternatives people use; the job is stable across solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a lens that reveals competition and context, and one that becomes abstract.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> an abstract job that justifies any feature; a job written from the desk; ignoring the emotional and social dimensions.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;when I prepare the monthly business review, I want to show my VP four numbers and a trend, so I look prepared&quot; for a B2B analytics product (Worked example 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Job map steps: define what they are trying to do; locate the information; prepare; confirm; execute; monitor; modify; conclude. Not every job has all eight steps. Use them as prompts.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-04 · The interview guide · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision to inform, a segment, and recruited participants.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a guide, a set of notes, and a theme summary with support counts.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> participants can recall specific past events; you can record or take notes.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> depth versus sample size; flexibility versus comparability.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> hypothetical questions; leading questions; pitching; recruiting only friendly users.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> eight interviews with account managers about the last monthly review produced a pattern of manual slide-building (Worked example 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Structure for a 45-minute interview: <strong>Open</strong> (5 minutes: purpose, consent, recording, no right answers) · <strong>Context</strong> (5: role, setting) · <strong>The last time</strong> (15: &quot;Tell me about the last time you did X,&quot; then the steps, tools, people, obstacles) · <strong>Struggles and workarounds</strong> (10: what was hard, what they do about it, what they tried) · <strong>Priorities</strong> (5: what would matter most) · <strong>Close</strong> (5: who else to talk to, thanks). Questions to avoid: &quot;Would you use...?&quot;, &quot;Don't you think...?&quot;, &quot;How much would you pay?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-05 · The survey design checklist · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a decision and a population.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a short survey, a sampling plan, an expected margin of error, and a list of known biases.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the right questions are known from prior qualitative work; a reasonable response rate is achievable.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> length versus completion; precision versus cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> leading wording; no decision attached; intention questions treated as behavior; ignoring who did not answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> a 400-person survey on willingness to pay gives a headline of 30% with a margin of about ±4.5 points, and a fake-door test shows a much lower real rate (Worked example 3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Checklist: (1) decision and what changes by answer; (2) population and how you sample; (3) question types, one idea each, neutral, balanced; (4) behavior over intention; (5) order: easy first, sensitive last; (6) length under about 10 minutes; (7) pilot with five; (8) expected response rate and margin of error (about 1 over the square root of the number of responses, in the worst case); (9) who is missing and which way it biases; (10) how you will check against behavior.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-06 · The feedback weighting table · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a set of feedback items from several sources.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a ranked list of themes with source mix, segment, frequency and severity, and a view of confidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can map each item to a segment and a source.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> weighting reduces the pull of loud voices and requires judgment.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> counting mentions without weighting by segment; treating the loudest source as representative; ignoring consistency across sources.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> a feature complaint appears in tickets and in interviews and in behavior data and ranks above a louder request that appears only on social media (Exercise EX-C06-07).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Theme</th>\n<th>Sources (tickets, interviews, behavior, social, sales)</th>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Severity</th>\n<th>Consistency across sources</th>\n<th>Confidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<p>Rule: a theme seen in three or more independent sources and in the target segment outranks a theme seen in one loud source.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-07 · The riskiest-assumption test · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an idea and a list of what must be true for it to work.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the riskiest assumption, a cheap test, a success criterion written in advance, and a decision for each outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the assumptions can be listed honestly; a cheap test exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed versus rigor; a cheap test can mislead if unrepresentative.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> testing the safest assumption; criteria set after the result; a test that measures opinion.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the two-week group-ordering plan in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) list assumptions in four groups (desirability, usability, feasibility, viability); (2) rate each for importance (what if false?) and uncertainty (how little do we know?); (3) pick the high-importance, high-uncertainty one; (4) choose the cheapest behavioral test; (5) write the criterion and the decision rule; (6) run, and record what you learned. (C15 extends this into assumption mapping.)</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c06-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C06-08 · The disappointment survey and segmentation engine · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> the survey question and the 40% benchmark come from Sean Ellis; the four-step engine (segment, analyze feedback, build the roadmap, repeat) is Rahul Vohra's, described in a First Round Review article (12 November 2018) <code>[V: First Round Review, 2018]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> users who have had a real chance to use the product (for example, recent active users), and a short survey.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the share who would be &quot;very disappointed&quot; to lose the product, the segment who love it, the reasons, and a roadmap that serves them.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the survey reaches users who have experienced the core value; the benchmark is a guide, not a law.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a clear number that can be moved and the risk of optimizing a survey score; the benchmark was set on other products.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> surveying the wrong users; treating 40% as a threshold that proves anything; ignoring non-respondents.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Superhuman's reported move from 22% to a much higher score (CS-C06-02).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps as reported: (1) ask &quot;How would you feel if you could no longer use the product?&quot; with &quot;very disappointed,&quot; &quot;somewhat disappointed,&quot; &quot;not disappointed&quot;; (2) segment the &quot;very disappointed&quot; group to find who the product is for; (3) read what that group loves and what holds back the &quot;somewhat disappointed&quot; users who value the main benefit; (4) build a roadmap that serves both; (5) repeat and track the score.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<p>Four question types use this chapter. The user steps inside design answers are covered in C07 and C08.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-type-a-how-would-you-research-x-or-plan-the-discovery\"></span>\n<h4>Type A: &quot;How would you research X?&quot; or &quot;Plan the discovery&quot;</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompts:</em> &quot;How would you research why students struggle to find housing near campus?&quot; &quot;How would you understand why people don't use our scheduling feature?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Structure</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify the decision</strong> the research will inform and the segment.</li>\n<li><strong>State hypotheses</strong> and the riskiest assumption.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose methods to fit the question:</strong> qualitative to find, quantitative to size, behavioral data to check (C06.5 to C06.7).</li>\n<li><strong>Plan the details:</strong> who to recruit, how many, the questions, and the biases you will watch.</li>\n<li><strong>State what you will do with the result:</strong> the decision rule and the criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Close</strong> with the timeline and what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Time budget for a ten-minute segment</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the decision and segment</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Decision, segment, constraints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypotheses and riskiest assumption</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Two or three hypotheses; the one to test first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methods</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>Qualitative, quantitative and behavioral, in order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan and biases</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>Sample, questions, biases, timeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision rule and close</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Criteria written in advance; what changes the plan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c06-type-b-users-want-feature-x-what-do-you-do\"></span>\n<h4>Type B: &quot;Users want feature X. What do you do?&quot;</h4>\n<p>Step up the ladder (FW-C06-01): ask what they are trying to do, what happens today, and how often. Segment: who is asking, and are they the target? Weigh the evidence (FW-C06-06): is the need visible in several sources? Offer solution classes, including a smaller one, and choose with a criterion. Close with a cheap test and what would change the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-type-c-customers-say-they-d-pay-for-y-do-you-believe-them\"></span>\n<h4>Type C: &quot;Customers say they'd pay for Y. Do you believe them?&quot;</h4>\n<p>Name the say-do gap, say how you would narrow it (behavioral evidence, a commitment test), estimate a discount, and size the decision on the discounted figure. Worked in Worked example 3 and IQ-C06-04.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-type-d-pick-a-user-segment-to-build-for\"></span>\n<h4>Type D: &quot;Pick a user segment to build for&quot;</h4>\n<p>Segment by need and behavior (C06.2), score with FW-C06-02, state the criterion and the segment you give up, and name the evidence you would collect to confirm. A full worked answer is IQ-C06-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Where to signpost.</strong> &quot;The decision this informs is...&quot; &quot;My riskiest assumption is...&quot; &quot;I'd start with interviews to find what to ask, then size it with a short survey, and check against behavior.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> One sentence: &quot;I'd run eight interviews and a fake-door test in two weeks, and I'd build only if at least X happens; I would change my plan if Y.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> State the decision before methods. Give an order for the methods and a reason. Name at least two biases specific to your plan, and one thing the research cannot tell you. If asked &quot;how many interviews?&quot;, answer with a reason (saturation per segment), not a number alone.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional and every figure is an invented assumption.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-worked-example-1-consumer-add-a-bedtime-mode-in-a-children-s\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer: &quot;add a bedtime mode&quot; in a children's video app (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The request.</strong> In one quarter, 47 of 3,100 store reviews (invented) ask for a &quot;bedtime mode.&quot; The team's first idea is a screen that dims and locks the app after a set time.</p>\n<p><strong>Step up the ladder (FW-C06-01).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature</td>\n<td>A bedtime mode that dims and locks the app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solution class</td>\n<td>Limiting viewing at night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem (to be confirmed)</td>\n<td>Parents struggle to end screen time without a fight, especially at bedtime</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Interviews.</strong> The team interviews eight parents of children aged three to six (invented), asking each about the last time they ended a viewing session at bedtime. Seven describe a recent argument. Their workarounds (they could name several): turning off the home network (five), hiding the tablet (three), setting a phone timer (four). The timer is the telling one. Asked why it did not work, three said the timer ends the video but not the protest: the child needs a warning and a sense of an ending.</p>\n<p><strong>Segment and job.</strong> The segment is parents of children aged three to six who use the app at bedtime. The job statement: &quot;When it is time for bed and my child is mid-video, I want to end the session without a fight, so I can keep the routine calm.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Reframed problem statement.</strong> &quot;Parents of three- to six-year-olds who use the app at bedtime end sessions by cutting the network or taking the device, because a hard stop causes distress; seven of eight interviewed described a recent argument.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Solution classes.</strong> A hard lock (the original idea, which matches the workarounds that parents already say cause fights); a gentle wind-down (a visible &quot;last video&quot; countdown and a calm ending animation); a parent schedule with a warning. The wind-down class fits the job better than the lock.</p>\n<p><strong>Cheapest test.</strong> A clickable prototype of the wind-down with five parents, with the criterion written in advance: at least four of five say they would use it tonight and describe how it changes the routine. Metric if built: the share of bedtime sessions that end without the parent intervening, with a <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> on session starts.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The request named a feature; the interviews found a problem and an alternative that the feature would have missed. The reviews were the signal that something existed. They were not a sample, so the interviews did the work.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-worked-example-2-b2b-give-us-custom-dashboards-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — B2B: &quot;give us custom dashboards&quot; (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The request.</strong> Twelve of 30 enterprise accounts (invented) ask for custom dashboards in a sales-analytics product. A builder would take a team of six about four months.</p>\n<p><strong>Interviews and observation.</strong> The PM interviews eight account managers who made requests (invented) and watches two of them prepare their monthly review. The pattern: they export four charts to a spreadsheet, tidy them, and paste them into slides for a VP. Six of the eight say it takes about two hours each month. None had tried building a custom view.</p>\n<p><strong>Job statement.</strong> &quot;When I prepare my monthly review, I want to show my VP four numbers and a trend, so I look prepared and don't spend a Sunday on slides.&quot; The functional job is a summary; the social job is looking prepared.</p>\n<p><strong>Segment check.</strong> The same requests came from two roles. Managers (who present) want the summary. Analysts (who explore) do want flexible dashboards. They are different segments with different jobs. The requests had been pooled.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Dashboard builder</td>\n<td>Flexibility for analysts</td>\n<td>Four months; managers may never use it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. One-click monthly slide export of the four most common charts</td>\n<td>The managers' job</td>\n<td>Two weeks; no help for analysts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Templated monthly summary email</td>\n<td>Speed to ship</td>\n<td>Less control for managers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Decision and test.</strong> Build B first for managers, and test with ten accounts, with the criterion set in advance: at least six use it in their next monthly review. Revisit the builder for analysts with a separate round of interviews.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Step up the ladder, find that one request hid two jobs, and match the solution to the job. Careful here: eight interviews cannot tell you how many accounts share the job; the ten-account test starts to answer that.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-worked-example-3-survey-would-you-pay-10-a-month-for-the-pre\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Survey: &quot;Would you pay $10 a month for the premium tier?&quot; (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> A productivity app considers a premium tier at $10 a month. It has 100,000 monthly active users (invented). A survey of 400 randomly sampled active users (invented) asks, &quot;Would you pay $10 a month for [description]?&quot; and 30% say yes.</p>\n<p><strong>Margin of error.</strong> For a proportion of 30% from 400 responses, the 95% margin is about 1.96 × the square root of (0.30 × 0.70 ÷ 400) = 1.96 × 0.0229 = about 4.5 points. So the survey says 30%, plausibly 25% to 35%. (The reasoning is C12's.)</p>\n<p><strong>The naive forecast.</strong> 30% of 100,000 is 30,000 payers; at $10 a month that is $3.6 million a year.</p>\n<p><strong>The say-do gap.</strong> Stated willingness to pay is a prediction of future behavior, which is exactly where the gap is largest. A commitment test narrows it.</p>\n<p><strong>A fake-door test.</strong> The app shows the premium offer to 5,000 users (invented) with a real checkout. Of those, 4% click &quot;upgrade&quot; (200 users) and 1.2% complete payment (60 users). The behavior-based rate is 1.2%.</p>\n<p><strong>The corrected forecast.</strong> 1.2% of 100,000 is 1,200 payers; at $120 a year that is $144,000 a year. The survey figure was 25 times too high (30% ÷ 1.2%). The ratio is itself a useful number: for this audience and this offer, stated intention overstated behavior by about 25 times (invented), so future surveys should be discounted by a factor close to that, and tested again.</p>\n<p><strong>Narrowing the uncertainty.</strong> The 1.2% comes from 5,000 exposed users, so it is itself an estimate, with a margin of about ±0.3 points (1.96 × the square root of (0.012 × 0.988 ÷ 5,000) = 0.3%). The range is 0.9% to 1.5%, or $108,000 to $180,000 a year, still nowhere near $3.6 million.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> At $144,000 a year the tier will not fund a team. Options: a lower price or an annual plan to test elasticity, a more compelling bundle, or stop. The cheap next test is a price test with the same checkout.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A survey gave a precise-looking number, and behavior gave a very different one. The commitment test cost two days and avoided planning around a figure 25 times too high.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-worked-example-4-research-plan-two-weeks-and-no-budget-for-a\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Research plan: two weeks and no budget for a group-ordering feature (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Setting.</strong> A food-delivery app is asked whether to build group ordering (a shared cart that several people add to). The team has two weeks and no research budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Assumptions listed (FW-C06-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Assumption</th>\n<th>Importance</th>\n<th>Uncertainty</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Desirability</td>\n<td>Groups regularly coordinate orders and find it hard</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Desirability</td>\n<td>A shared cart, not a chat, is the right fix</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Viability</td>\n<td>Groups order often enough to matter</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feasibility</td>\n<td>A shared cart is buildable in a quarter</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The riskiest is the first one: if groups do not struggle, nothing else matters.</p>\n<p><strong>The plan (14 days).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Days 1 to 3: use what exists.</em> Count orders with three or more items sent to one address at one time. This is 4% of orders (invented), a rough proxy for group orders. Read 100 support tickets (invented) for coordination complaints.</li>\n<li><em>Days 3 to 8: eight interviews</em> with customers who placed large orders in the past month, asking about the last time they ordered for a group.</li>\n<li><em>Days 6 to 12: a fake door.</em> Show a &quot;Start a group order&quot; button to 3% of users who have ordered three or more items, leading to a &quot;coming soon, tell us what you need&quot; page with a short form.</li>\n<li><em>Days 10 to 14: a concierge test.</em> Coordinate ten group orders by hand through messages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Criteria, written before the test.</strong> At least five of eight interviewees describe a recent coordination struggle. The fake door gets at least 4% of exposed users clicking. At least seven of ten concierge orders complete with three or more participants.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision rule.</strong> All three met: build a thin version. Two met: iterate on the concept and re-test. One or none: stop.</p>\n<p><strong>What the plan cannot tell us.</strong> How group ordering performs at scale, and whether a built version would match the hand-coordinated experience. It cheaply tests desirability, which is the assumption most likely to be wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Discovery with a deadline: the riskiest assumption first, several cheap behavioral tests, and a decision rule fixed in advance.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Your team believes college students struggle to find affordable housing near campus. How would you research it before building anything?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c06-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd run a survey of students asking what they want in a housing app, and look at what features competitors have. Then I'd build the features that come up the most.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no decision, no segments, one method, intentions treated as evidence, and the result is a feature list.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd clarify what decision this informs: whether to build a housing product at all, and for whom. 'Students' are several segments with different needs: first-year students who need on-campus or nearby housing quickly, international students who cannot visit before arriving, and graduate students who want quieter, longer-term places. I'd start with one or two.</p>\n<p>&quot;My hypothesis is that the problem differs by segment, and that the biggest pain is trust and speed for those who cannot visit. I'd start with qualitative work: eight interviews per segment about the last time they searched, what they used, where it went wrong and what they did about it. Then I'd size it with a short survey, built from what the interviews showed, to find how common each problem is. And I'd check against behavior, such as how many listings students contact and how long the search takes.</p>\n<p>&quot;Before I start I'd write the decision rule: for example, build only if at least a third of the segment reports a serious problem and there is a workaround they already pay for or invest time in.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c06-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd name my riskiest assumption: that students can't find places, rather than that places they can afford don't exist. If the problem is price, a search product will not fix it. The cheapest first check is a conversation: ask people to walk me through their last search, and watch one do it live, because what people skip or tolerate in a search shows more than what they tell me.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'm aware of the say-do gap. If students tell me they'd pay for verified listings, I'd test with a commitment: a landing page with real listings and an alert sign-up, and a refundable deposit to hold a viewing slot with a few landlords.</p>\n<p>&quot;For bias, if I recruit through the university, I'll over-sample engaged students and miss those who gave up searching, so I'd also recruit through the places they ask for help. Interviews can't tell me how many students have the problem, so the survey does that, and the survey can't tell me why, so the interviews do that.</p>\n<p>&quot;Timeline: three weeks to a decision. What would change my plan: if the main pain turns out to be price or landlord behavior rather than search, I would stop and look at a different kind of solution, such as a verified-landlord program.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c06-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Names the decision and segments</td>\n<td>Names the riskiest assumption and tests it first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses qualitative, then quantitative, then behavioral evidence</td>\n<td>Observes behavior, and uses commitment tests for willingness to pay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writes a decision rule in advance</td>\n<td>Names biases specific to the plan and what the research cannot tell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives a timeline</td>\n<td>Says what finding would make you stop</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c06-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google has long published a stated philosophy that starts with focusing on the user <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, Ten things we know to be true]</code>. Candidates commonly report that product rounds start with who the user is and what their pain is <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: spend your first minutes on a specific user and a ranked problem, not on features.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes Customer Obsession as a leadership principle <code>Public</code>, and accounts by former leaders describe the practice of writing a press release and FAQ before building, called working backwards <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon working-backwards material (AWS re:Invent 2022 deck; Fast Company)]</code>. Practical consequence: state the customer problem in the customer's words and describe the experience you would want them to have.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report product-sense rounds that expect a specific user and a quantified problem, with follow-ups on how you would know <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: pair each user claim with a way to size it, and be ready for &quot;how do you know that is a problem for many people?&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft has publicly described being customer-obsessed as part of its culture <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>, and enterprise products involve buyers who are not the daily users <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: distinguish the buyer, the administrator and the daily user, and say whose problem you are solving.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple publishes design principles and platform guidelines <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines (developer.apple.com)]</code>. Commentary often states that Apple relies on taste and integrated design more than on stated customer wishes, and some commonly cited remarks attributed to its late co-founder express skepticism of asking customers what they want <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: show that you observe behavior and trace problems to experience, and avoid presenting a survey as the main evidence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material emphasizes judgment and context <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report data-informed product questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: combine qualitative insight with behavioral evidence, and be explicit about what each cannot show.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's users are developers and the businesses that employ them <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe documentation and newsroom]</code>. Candidates report discovery prompts about developer tools <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: observe developers integrating for the first time instead of asking what they want, and separate the person who integrates from the person who buys.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c06-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Taking a feature request at face value</td>\n<td>Your plan is to build what was asked</td>\n<td>Step up the ladder: what are they trying to do?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A demographic user</td>\n<td>&quot;College students&quot; or &quot;millennials&quot;</td>\n<td>Segment by need and behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Hypothetical questions in interviews</td>\n<td>&quot;Would you use...?&quot;</td>\n<td>Ask about the last time it happened</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Leading or pitching</td>\n<td>You describe your idea before listening</td>\n<td>Ask open questions and hold the pitch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Trusting stated intentions</td>\n<td>You forecast from &quot;I would pay&quot;</td>\n<td>Use behavior or a commitment test and discount</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A research plan of only one method</td>\n<td>&quot;Run a survey&quot;</td>\n<td>Qualitative to find, quantitative to size, behavior to check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Sampling friendly users</td>\n<td>Everyone you interviewed loves the product</td>\n<td>Recruit across the segment, including lapsed users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A problem statement with a solution in it</td>\n<td>It contains &quot;needs a button&quot;</td>\n<td>Remove the solution words and re-test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Testing the safe assumption</td>\n<td>The test could not have told you no</td>\n<td>Test the high-importance, high-uncertainty one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Discovery with no decision rule</td>\n<td>You cannot say what would make you stop</td>\n<td>Write criteria before the test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c06-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> and <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> come from C02, and the streetlight effect and the map-territory idea from C03 and C05.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). Ask how you would guarantee that you build the wrong thing: interview only fans, ask hypothetical questions, pitch while you listen, write the criterion after the result. The answers are the checklist for good discovery.</p>\n<p><strong>The streetlight effect</strong> (<code>MM-streetlight</code>, from C03) is searching where it is easy to look. In research it is interviewing the customers who are easiest to reach (current, friendly, nearby) and surveying the channel you already have. The people who left, or never came, are where the answer often is. Recruit for them on purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>Extreme users</strong> (<code>MM-extreme-users</code>) is the practice of studying people at the edges of a segment, who show needs that typical users have more mildly. A person who navigates the app with a screen reader exposes unclear labels that everyone stumbles on a little. A heavy user shows what breaks at scale. It is a cheap way to see a problem sharply, as a source of hypotheses rather than a replacement for a typical sample.</p>\n<p><strong>The map is not the territory</strong> (<code>MM-map-territory</code>, see C05). A persona or a journey map is a map of users, and what it leaves out is invisible. A persona of &quot;Busy Priya, 34, loves yoga&quot; tells a team nothing about when she abandons a checkout. Use a persona as a prompt to look, and date it.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers are users of your product's constraints. Bring one to interviews: they spot feasibility facts early (&quot;that field isn't stored&quot;) and they hear the problem directly, which reduces the translation loss between a spec and what gets built.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers and researchers own methods you should not improvise away. Co-write research questions, ask them what would change the plan, and watch sessions rather than reading summaries. A designer's prototype is often the cheapest test (C06.9).</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Analytics tells you what happened and a survey tells you how many say so; both need a view of who is missing. Ask data science for the baseline behavior before you interview, so that you can ask people about the numbers that surprise you.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c06-checklist-before-you-commit-to-a-problem\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you commit to a problem</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the problem written without a solution, with a specific segment and situation?</li>\n<li>Is there evidence from behavior, not only opinion?</li>\n<li>Did I hear it from people in the target segment, including some who left?</li>\n<li>Do I know how many people have it, even roughly?</li>\n<li>Have I seen what they do about it today?</li>\n<li>Is my riskiest assumption identified and tested cheaply?</li>\n<li>Is the success criterion written down before the test?</li>\n<li>Do I know what would change my mind?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c06-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c06-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C06-01 · The fitness app that wanted more variety · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A fitness app loses about half of its new users within a month. The product team's belief is that users leave because there is not enough workout variety, and the plan is to commission 200 new workouts over three months.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A fixed content budget for the quarter and a design team of three. The leadership team wants a decision in two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> An exit survey with options written by the team shows 41% of 600 respondents choosing &quot;not enough variety&quot; (invented). A review of behavior data shows that 62% of lapses begin after a user misses a week of workouts (invented). The team interviews ten lapsed users (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The exit survey options came from the team's own idea, so the survey could only confirm it. The interviews and behavior data point to a different problem: users who miss a week do not return.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Seven of the ten interviewees stopped after missing a week through illness, travel or a busy period. They describe feeling guilty about the broken streak and avoiding opening the app, and some restarted months later with a different app at the start of a new year. A workaround they mention is deleting the app and reinstalling it so the streak resets. None says they ran out of workouts. The survey and the behavior data seem to disagree until you see the survey options: &quot;variety&quot; was the closest option for people who felt the app no longer fit them. The problem for this segment is re-entry after a gap, not variety. The riskiest assumption for the new idea is that people would return if re-entry felt easier, which a cheap test can check.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. 200 new workouts</td>\n<td>The team's original belief</td>\n<td>A quarter of content budget; may not touch the cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. A gentle restart flow: a welcome-back message, a ten-minute workout, and a streak freeze for illness or travel</td>\n<td>Return after a gap</td>\n<td>Two weeks of design and engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Both</td>\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Slower; confounds the learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs far less and is directly aimed at the observed cause. It gives up the possibility that variety also matters for a different segment, which the team can test later.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Build B, and run it for users who have missed seven days, with a holdout. The metric is the share of lapsed users who return within 14 days. A guardrail is the completion rate of the first workout back, and a counter metric is the rate of users who freeze a streak and never return.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the return rate within 14 days after a missed week rose from 18% to 27% (invented), and the content budget was kept for a smaller variety test with the segment of users who had exhausted the beginner plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the return rate had not moved, the team would have returned to interviews to ask what stopped lapsed users from using the welcome-back message. If the variety explanation had been right for another segment, a separate test would have shown it. The lesson is the method: when your survey options came from your own hypothesis, interviews and behavior data are what can contradict it.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c06-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C06-02 · Superhuman, 2017: measuring and raising product-market fit · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): a first-person account by the company's founder and CEO, Rahul Vohra, &quot;How Superhuman Built an Engine to Find Product/Market Fit,&quot; First Round Review (12 November 2018), read in full (primary); Hiten Shah's 2015 Slack survey is known only through that article. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> Superhuman was building an email product and had been building it since 2015 and, by the summer of 2017 with a team of 14, had still not launched widely <code>[V: Vohra, 2018]</code>. In 2017 its founder wanted an objective way to tell whether the product had product-market fit, rather than debating it on feeling.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A small team, a product still unlaunched after two years that the founder chose not to push into growth early, and no established metric for fit that worked before launch <code>[V: Vohra, 2018]</code>. He had reviewed available advice and settled on a survey approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> He used a survey question associated with Sean Ellis: &quot;How would you feel if you could no longer use this product?&quot; with answers &quot;very disappointed,&quot; &quot;somewhat disappointed&quot; and &quot;not disappointed.&quot; Ellis's observation, as reported, is that companies with strong traction almost always had more than 40% of users answer &quot;very disappointed.&quot; Vohra reports that in the summer of 2017 only 22% of Superhuman's surveyed users answered &quot;very disappointed.&quot; He cites a figure of 51% for Slack, from Hiten Shah's 2015 open survey of 731 Slack users, taken when Slack had around half a million paying users, as an example of how hard the 40% benchmark is to beat. Ellis found the 40% figure after benchmarking nearly a hundred startups. The survey was sent to users who had used the product at least twice in the last two weeks, following Ellis's advice; at the time Superhuman had between 100 and 200 such users to poll <code>[V: Vohra, 2018]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The score was below the benchmark. The question was how to raise it systematically, not by guessing.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> As described, the approach has four parts. First, segment the responses to find who loves the product (the &quot;very disappointed&quot; group) and who it is for; this is a way to find the high-expectation customer. Second, read what that group says they love, which tells you what to protect. Third, read what the &quot;somewhat disappointed&quot; group says is holding them back, focusing on those who value the main benefit (so the product is still what they came for, but something blocks them), and treat the objections of those who do not value the benefit as noise to set aside. Fourth, turn both into the roadmap, in roughly even shares as he describes, and measure again, repeating the cycle. In his account half of the roadmap went to deepening what the loving users valued (speed, shortcuts, automation, design details) and half to what held back the speed-loving but &quot;somewhat disappointed&quot; users (a mobile app, integrations, attachment handling, calendaring, a unified inbox, better search, read receipts), and the score became the product team's single key result <code>[V: Vohra, 2018]</code>. His own numbering of the steps is: segment, analyze feedback, build the roadmap, repeat. In the terms of this chapter, it is a loop that uses a behavioral-attitude measure, segmentation, qualitative reading of open-text answers, and a cheap repeatable test, with the decision rule fixed in advance (build to raise the score).</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep debating whether the product fits</td>\n<td>Nothing measurable</td>\n<td>Time; decisions by feeling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Measure with the survey and run the segmentation loop</td>\n<td>Evidence and a number that can move</td>\n<td>Survey fatigue; the benchmark may not transfer; a survey score can be gamed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Listen to every complaint equally</td>\n<td>Broad feedback</td>\n<td>A scattered roadmap</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B narrows the product toward the users who love it, which can reduce reach. It also gives up the loudest requests from people the product is not for. It replaces an unmeasurable argument with a measurable, if imperfect, number.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Vohra adopted the survey as a metric and the segmentation loop as a process.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported by Vohra: the score was 22% when the work began in the summer of 2017; it was 33% after segmenting the same responses to the users who loved the product most (a change in who was counted, not in the product); and within three quarters of product work it nearly doubled to 58%. He also reports that the team grew to 22 people and that the company's Net Promoter Score rose alongside the fit score <code>[V: Vohra, 2018]</code>. He cautions that the score can fall as a product reaches more demanding users. Whether the score itself caused the growth that followed is a separate question that this case does not settle.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the survey reached users who had not yet experienced the core value, the score would have understated fit. Had the team broadened the product to please the &quot;not disappointed&quot; group, the segment that loved it might have been diluted. A risk is optimizing the survey score rather than the experience; a counter metric such as retention of the surveyed users checks it.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson for an interview answer.</strong> Turn a feeling (&quot;do we have fit?&quot;) into a measurable question, find the segment that loves the product, and use the people who almost love it as the source of the roadmap. Say what the number cannot tell you: here, the benchmark came from other products, and a rising score is not proof of causation.</p>\n<span id=\"c06-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption. Each is written against the five behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-01 · Beginner · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Customers keep asking us to add a &quot;bulk edit&quot; feature to our project-management tool. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, since customers are asking,&quot; or &quot;ask customers what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Steps up from the request to the job, checks who is asking, weighs the evidence, and offers more than one solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates segments among the requesters, uses behavior as well as requests, proposes the cheapest test, and says what would make the answer no.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat &quot;bulk edit&quot; as a solution and look for the problem under it. I'd ask the people who requested it to walk me through the last time they needed to change many items at once: what were they doing, how many items, how often, what did they do instead? Common workarounds are exporting to a spreadsheet and re-importing, or editing items one by one.</p>\n<p>Then I'd check who is asking. If the requests come mostly from team leads managing hundreds of tasks, it is a different segment from a casual user with a dozen tasks. I'd look at behavior: how many users perform repeated single edits in a row (a signal that bulk edit would help), and what share of tickets or sales notes mention it.</p>\n<p>Suppose the interviews show the real job is &quot;after a planning meeting, I want to move twenty tasks to a new sprint and reassign owners, so I can leave with everything in order.&quot; Bulk edit is one solution. Others are a &quot;move a group&quot; action, a planning view that allows drag and drop, or a template for sprint planning. They differ in cost, and the narrower ones may serve the job.</p>\n<p>My recommendation is to ship the narrowest version that serves the job for the team-lead segment, and test it with five customers who asked, with the criterion that at least four use it in their next planning meeting. The cost is that other bulk operations wait. I'd change my mind if the behavior data show that few users perform repeated edits, in which case the requests are loud but not widespread.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The requesters are your largest customers and sales says the feature is blocking two renewals. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would take the commercial fact seriously and still test the need. I would ask sales for the accounts and the value at risk, say two renewals worth $240,000, and have a call with each customer about the last time they needed to change many items. Then I would look for a smaller step that solves the stated job: multi-select with three actions might cover most of what they do. I would compare the cost of that step with the revenue at risk and commit to a date, but I would not promise the full feature before I know what the job requires, because a promise made to close a deal is hard to reverse.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test the cheapest version before building anything? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a fake door and a concierge step. Add an Edit selected button that appears when a user has made five or more single edits in a row, and when clicked shows a short message offering a spreadsheet-import workaround and a request to join a pilot. Measure the click rate among eligible users: proceed if at least 10% click and at least three of five follow-up interviews describe a repeated need; stop if under 3%. For the pilot users I would do the bulk change by hand, which shows exactly which actions are needed and how often.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-02 · Beginner · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A language-learning app serves students, working professionals and retirees. Which segment would you build for first?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Students, since there are the most of them,&quot; with no criterion.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by need, scores on stated criteria, chooses one, and names what it gives up.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notes the segments may be defined by a job (not an age), tests the choice with evidence, and says what would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll first note that age is a weak guide to need. What matters is why each person is learning, so I'd restate the segments by job: students learning for exams, professionals learning for a move or a role abroad, and retirees learning for travel and enjoyment. I'll score them on size, pain intensity, reach and our right to win (FW-C06-02), with rough assumed values.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment (by job)</th>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Pain intensity</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Fit and right to win</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exam learners</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium (deadline, but school provides structure)</td>\n<td>Medium (crowded channels)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professionals relocating</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High (a date, real stakes, willingness to pay)</td>\n<td>High (can reach through employers and expat forums)</td>\n<td>High (needs practical speaking, which the product can deliver)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retirees learning for travel</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low to medium (no deadline)</td>\n<td>Low (different channels)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I choose professionals who are relocating. The deciding criterion is pain intensity combined with reach: they have a deadline and they pay, and we can reach them directly. I give up the size of the exam-learner segment and the long-term loyalty of retirees.</p>\n<p>Evidence I'd collect to confirm: interviews with eight people who relocated in the past year about how they prepared, which tools they used and what failed; the share who paid for tutoring; and a landing-page test for a &quot;speak at work in 30 days&quot; offer. I'd change my choice if interviews showed that relocating professionals mostly rely on employer-provided classes, which would shrink our opening.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you validate that relocating professionals are the right first segment? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By testing the two things the table assumed: willingness to pay and reach. I would run ten interviews with people who recently moved for work, asking what they spent on language learning and why, and run a small paid-ad test with a price shown on the sign-up page, measuring the cost per sign-up and the share who start a trial. I would also look at our own data: among users who signed up citing relocation, is retention better than for others? If this group is willing to pay more and acquires at a cost our price can cover, the choice holds; if not, I would look at the next segment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would make you choose a different segment? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Payback. If acquiring professionals costs $60 per paying user and they pay $12 a month with 8% monthly churn, payback is five months and lifetime value is about $150. If exam learners cost $15 to acquire through school and referral channels, pay $6, and churn at 10%, payback is under three months, even though lifetime value is lower. I would choose the segment that reaches payback fastest at a volume we can serve, and keep the first segment's bigger prize as the second step.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-03 · Intermediate · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> People don't use our calendar app's scheduling assistant. How would you find out why?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Survey users to ask why they don't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments non-users, lists hypotheses, combines behavioral data, interviews and a survey, and defines the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates &quot;never saw it,&quot; &quot;tried and left&quot; and &quot;doesn't need it,&quot; sets a decision rule, and addresses sampling bias against people who have already left.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> First the decision: do we invest in the assistant, change how it is discovered, or retire it? I'd also define &quot;don't use&quot;: say 4% of weekly users have used it in the last month (assumed), against an expectation of 15%.</p>\n<p>The non-users are three different groups, and the research should separate them: people who never saw the assistant (discovery), people who tried it and stopped (a usability or value problem), and people who looked and decided they don't need it (a need problem). Each leads to a different action.</p>\n<p>Behavioral data comes first and is cheap: what share of weekly users saw the entry point, what share of those started it, what share of starters completed a scheduled meeting, and what share used it again. Say exposure is 20%, start rate 30% of those, completion 50% of starters. Then a user who sees it has a 15% chance of finishing once (0.30 × 0.50), and only 20% see it, so overall 3% finish (0.20 × 0.15), close to the observed 4%. The biggest loss is exposure.</p>\n<p>Interviews come second: eight people from each group, asking them to describe the last time they scheduled a meeting with several people, how they did it, and what they tried. For the &quot;tried and left&quot; group, I'd ask them to show me what happened. A short survey third, built from the interviews, would size how common each reason is.</p>\n<p>Sampling is the danger: people who left the app are not in my active-user list, so I'd recruit some lapsed users separately. My decision rule: if the main loss is exposure and those who try it complete meetings, fix discovery; if many who try it abandon, fix the experience; if most say they don't schedule multi-person meetings, retire it. I'd change the plan if the need turns out to be concentrated in a segment such as team admins, and I'd focus on them.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you include people who have already left your product in the research? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would recruit them from the event logs: users who started the assistant once and did not use it again in 60 days, and users who saw the entry point and never clicked. I would offer a small incentive and ask for a 15-minute call, and compare what they say with active users'. People who left do not answer surveys inside the product, so a survey alone would be biased toward people who stayed. For the group that never saw the entry point I would also review a few anonymized session recordings, since they cannot tell me what they did not see.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is your decision rule after the research? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Written before the interviews. If more than 60% of non-users never saw the entry point, the problem is discovery and I would test a different placement. If more than 40% of those who tried it left because suggestions were poor, the problem is quality and I would fix suggestions before promoting it. If most say they do not schedule meetings often enough to need it, the problem is need and I would retire or narrow the feature. With interview samples of 10 to 15 these are rough thresholds, so I would confirm the leading cause with the behavioral funnel.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-04 · Intermediate · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> In a survey, 35% of customers say they would pay $15 a month for a new feature. Do you believe it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, 35% is a strong signal,&quot; or &quot;No, surveys are unreliable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the say-do gap, computes the margin of error, asks about sampling, and proposes a behavioral test.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Estimates a discount factor, tests with a commitment, converts the result into a revenue range, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat the 35% as the upper bound of interest, not a forecast, for three reasons.</p>\n<p>First, it is a stated intention about future behavior, which is where the say-do gap is largest. People say yes to hypothetical purchases because saying yes costs nothing.</p>\n<p>Second, sampling: how many responded and who? Suppose 300 responded out of 2,000 invited (assumed), a 15% response rate, so the respondents are more interested than the typical customer. The margin of error on 35% from 300 responses is about 1.96 × the square root of (0.35 × 0.65 ÷ 300) = about 5.4 points, so the survey says 30% to 40% of respondents, before any non-response bias.</p>\n<p>Third, price and framing: how was the feature described, and was the price shown at the moment of choice?</p>\n<p>To get a behavioral number, I'd run a commitment test: show the offer with a real checkout to a sample, or take refundable pre-orders. Suppose 5,000 users see the offer (assumed) and 1.5% pay. Then the working conversion is about 1.5%, and the stated-to-actual ratio is about 23 (35% ÷ 1.5%). I'd use that ratio, labeled as specific to this audience and offer, to discount future surveys.</p>\n<p>For 50,000 active customers (assumed), 1.5% is 750 payers, at $15 a month that is $135,000 a year. The survey implied 17,500 payers, or $3.15 million a year, so the forecast error would have been more than twenty-fold.</p>\n<p>I'd decide on the behavioral number, and I'd say what would change the plan: a price test or a different packaging that raises conversion above about 4%, which is the level at which the feature covers a small team (assumed).</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What discount would you apply to the 35%, and why? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would not guess; I would calibrate. If the team has a past launch where a survey said 30% would pay and actual conversion was 6%, the discount factor is 0.2, and I would apply it to this survey. Combined with the 15% response rate, the revenue range for 100,000 customers is 100,000 × 35% × 0.2 = 7,000 payers, or $1.26 million a year at $15 a month; at a harsher factor of 0.1 it is about $0.63 million. I would present a range of roughly $0.6 to $1.3 million and say the first thing to learn is the real conversion from a commitment test.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you test willingness to pay with a commitment? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By asking for something costly. A fake door in the product that shows the feature at $15 a month with a Start trial button, shown to a random 10,000 users for two weeks, measures real clicks; a pre-order or a trial that requires a payment method is stronger. I would set the threshold in advance: if at least 3% of exposed users start the trial, proceed to a prototype; if under 1%, stop. Fake doors need care: the next screen should be honest that the feature is coming, and I would limit exposure.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c06-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C06-05 · Hard · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You have two weeks and no research budget. How would you decide whether to build a group-ordering feature in a food-delivery app?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Survey customers and see if they want it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists assumptions, picks the riskiest, designs two or three cheap tests, and writes criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Combines existing data, interviews and a commitment test, writes a decision rule in advance, and names what the plan cannot tell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd list what must be true: that groups regularly coordinate orders and find it hard (desirability); that a shared cart is a better fix than what they do today; that groups order often enough to matter (viability); and that it is buildable (feasibility). The riskiest is the first, because if groups do not struggle, the rest does not matter.</p>\n";
